course: dictionary
lesson: 3455
title: Old spellings, magic words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words look old-fashioned because their spelling is old. «Faery» is an old or poetic spelling of «fairy». It still means a small magical creature, but the spelling makes it sound like something from a very old tale.
explain: Read the words around it to be sure what it means. In «a gleaming carpet of faery», a carpet covers the ground and gleaming means shining, so faery must be a place: fairyland, the magical land of fairies.
explain: Poets and writers choose words like this to create a feeling. When you meet faery, ask: is it a creature, a land, or just something magical? The words around it will tell you which.
fact: Faery is an old or poetic spelling of fairy.
fact: As a noun, faery can mean a small magical creature, or the land of fairies, which is called fairyland.
fact: As an adjective, faery means magical, or of fairies, as in «a faery glow».
fact: Faery is found in poems, old tales and fantasy books, where it makes things sound older and more mysterious.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Rat and Mole meet the Piper at the Gates of Dawn, a magical, almost fairy-like moment.
exercise: The snow made the wood «a gleaming carpet of faery». What does faery mean here? | A wet, grey day | A small brown bird | A cold, white snowflake | *Fairyland, the magical land of fairies
exercise: Which spelling is the old, poetic one of fairy? | ferry | fairy | fiery | *faery
exercise: A writer says «a faery glow» over a lake. What does faery mean in this phrase? | Cold and wet | Loud | Muddy | *Magical, of fairies
test: «The realm of Faery» is a phrase from an old book. Which meaning fits faery here? | A rough, hard surface | *The land of fairies | A tiny creature with wings | A type of wet weather
test: Why do poets sometimes write faery instead of fairy? | *To make things sound older and more mysterious | To say it is a spelling mistake | To mean the opposite of magic | Because it is shorter
exam: Which word is an adjective in «a faery glow over the lake»? | *faery | glow | over | lake
